🪟 When you need a Windows .ico file
Windows uses a special icon format: .ico  
It’s not optional — Windows apps must have one.

You need a .ico file in these situations:

1. If TotalRecalls has a Windows desktop app
Your installer and executable require:

app.ico

installer.ico

uninstaller.ico

Without it, Windows will show a generic blank icon.

2. If you want a proper Windows Start Menu tile
Windows Start Menu tiles use .ico files with multiple resolutions:

16×16

32×32

48×48

64×64

128×128

256×256

A single .ico file contains all of these sizes.

3. If you want a custom icon for your .exe
Any compiled Windows binary (Python → EXE, Rust, Go, C#, etc.) needs a .ico file embedded.

4. If you want correct icons in Windows Explorer
Without a .ico, your app shows:

a blank page icon

or a generic executable icon

With a .ico, you get:

crisp icons at all sizes

proper scaling

correct appearance in File Explorer, taskbar, and alt‑tab

